{"name":"Mountain Trail","hex":"#615742","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/500px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/1280px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/500px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mountain bike trail in the Forest of Dean, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountainBikeTrail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG/500px-Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG/1280px-Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG?utm_source=e